  "findings": [
    "Foreground is a request/planning-role label, not an assertion that code runs on the server main thread. The spawn worker inherits the foreground request ContextVar; explicitly bound prefetch threads override it.",
    "Session.prefetch calls self.read_barrier on the original shared Session before constructing store.reader_clone. That barrier can start a transaction on the main PgStore from the background thread.",
    "Observed BG BEGIN/SET on1149158 happens before main DISCARD ALL and thus is not a simple already-released pooled handoff. PgStore per-instance RLock serializes the observed shared-store statements.",
    "Original request-thread COMMIT then DISCARD is consistent with response finalization/Session close. Native context_close interval contains the DISCARD. The exact caller stack for COMMIT is not retained; finalization calls acommit as well as Session.close committing.",
    "Actual background payload SELECT is on the distinct clone connection/backend1149191 after main DISCARD. No cross-thread SQL send-to-ready overlap is found on1149158 within this selected request.",
    "Session.close waits for process readers only, while ordinary aggressive thread workers may remain active through native context close. Pool reuse is possible in general; no checkout/lease events were captured, so backend identity alone does not establish lease ownership."
  ],
